.\"
.\" THIS FILE IS THE SOURCE, not a generated artefact. Packaging substitutes
.\" @VERSION@ and @DATE@ and installs the result; nothing else in it is derived.
.\" It was a heredoc inside packaging/linux/payload.sh until it acquired content
.\" worth gating, and a gate cannot check a string that exists only during a
.\" build (ScrAP-123 is the same species one level up: a fact restated in prose,
.\" with nothing comparing it to its source).
.\"
.\" DESCRIPTION, COPYRIGHT and CREDITS deliberately restate what the running
.\" application says about itself in Help > About and its Credits tabs
.\" (src/app/appactions.rs, add_about_action), so a reader with no display gets
.\" the same attribution a reader with one does. The credit sections are a
.\" LICENCE OBLIGATION, not a courtesy (POLICY, Third-party attribution).
.\" `cargo xtask lint-references` check 17 holds the two texts in step.
.TH SCRIBOBULATE 1 "@DATE@" "scribobulate @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scribobulate \- native Markdown viewer and editor that renders on the CPU
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scribobulate
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.RI [ FILE ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Scribobulate
is a native Markdown viewer and editor, purpose-built for managing Markdown
generated by clankers.
.PP
It renders Markdown into native GTK4 widgets and forces the GSK Cairo software
renderer, so the process holds no GL context and no video memory. There is no
GPU, no WebEngine and no web stack anywhere in it. In an era where VRAM is
precious real estate for running local models, Scribobulate stays out of the
way.
.PP
Each
.I FILE
is opened in a tab. Opening a document while the application is already running
reuses that instance rather than starting a second process; see
.B SINGLE INSTANCE
below.
.PP
Supported throughout: live reload of externally edited files, split editing, a
document outline, preview reading themes, find and replace, CriticMarkup
annotation review, export to HTML and PDF, and crash recovery from an
unsaved-work snapshot.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-new\-instance
Force a separate process instead of reusing the running one \(em useful for
running a development build alongside an installed one. The remaining arguments
are still treated as files to open.
.IP
This switch is scanned out of the command line before the application registers
itself, which is why it does not appear in
.BR \-\-help :
by the time GLib parses options, the decision it controls has already been made.
.TP
.B \-\-probe\-startup
Print a startup marker and exit 0 without opening a window. It is a packaging
self-check \(em reaching this point proves the executable resolved its entire
shared-library graph \(em and is not useful interactively.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the standard GLib option summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help\-all
Show every option group, including GApplication's.
.TP
.B \-\-gapplication\-service
Enter GApplication service mode. Intended for use from a D-Bus service file, not
from a shell.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log filter. The application's own diagnostics and GTK's share one sink, so one
value controls both \(em for example
.BR info ,
.BR scribobulate=debug ,
or
.BR warn,scribobulate::scroll=trace .
Defaults to
.BR warn .
.TP
.B GSK_RENDERER
Read by GTK, but
.B set unconditionally to
.B cairo
by the application before GTK initialises. A value inherited from the
environment is overwritten, deliberately: holding no GPU context is the reason
this program exists, and leaving that switchable would make the guarantee a
suggestion.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base for the user configuration directory. Defaults to
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.BR XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_DIRS
Searched, in that order, for an installed
.I themes.toml
(see
.BR scribobulate (5)).
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Base for the state directory that holds the session, the log, crash reports and
crash-recovery snapshots. Defaults to
.IR ~/.local/state .
.SH FILES
.SS Configuration
.TP
.I ~/.config/scribobulate/config.toml
Window, margin, code-font and outline settings.
.TP
.I ~/.config/scribobulate/themes.toml
User overrides for preview reading themes, merged over the installed themes per
theme id and per key.
.TP
.I /usr/share/scribobulate/themes.toml
The installed reading themes.
.PP
Both files are documented in
.BR scribobulate (5).
A malformed one is reported and ignored; neither can prevent the application
from starting.
.SS State
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/scribobulate/session.toml
Open documents, window geometry and the selected theme, restored at startup.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/scribobulate/scribobulate.log
Persistent log. Every record at
.B info
or worse is written here regardless of
.BR RUST_LOG .
The previous run's log is kept as
.IR scribobulate.log.1 .
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/scribobulate/crash-*.log
A crash report, written by the panic and fatal-signal handlers. The application
announces one left by a previous run. Reports are never deleted automatically.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/scribobulate/swap/
Crash-recovery snapshots of unsaved edits, one
.I .swap
file per modified document, offered back on the next start.
.SS Other
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/scribobulate/THIRD\-PARTY\-LICENSES.md
Verbatim licence notices for the components compiled into the binary. See
.B CREDITS
below.
.SH SINGLE INSTANCE
The first launch registers the application ID
.B com.extollit.scribobulate
on the D\-Bus session bus. A later launch finds that registration, hands its file
arguments to the running process and exits, so one process holds every window and
every document. Passing
.B \-\-new\-instance
opts out of the negotiation for that launch only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on a clean shutdown and a non-zero status if GTK could not be
initialised \(em most commonly because no display is available.
.SH NOTES
On GTK versions before 4.12 the application redirects its own
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
to a temporary directory of symbolic links for the lifetime of the process, to
avoid a crash in GTK's compose-table loader on a large
.IR ~/.XCompose .
Configuration and themes are read before the redirect and are unaffected. The
workaround is inert on GTK 4.12 and later.
